<DocLayout title="Examples" eyebrow="Practical usage" description="Copyable workflows for live updates, reproducible traces, mode comparisons, and benchmark interpretation." next={{ label: "Read the architecture", href: "/architecture/" }}>
  <div class="doc-callout"><p><strong>Choose deliberately.</strong> Use <code>basic</code> for the recommended integration path. The <code>multilevel</code> recipe below is for research and evaluation while its complete paper-level guarantees remain under validation.</p></div>

  <h2>Maintain a live stream</h2>
  <p>Keep the matcher as the owner of graph state. Feed it updates in order and inspect the matching after each operation.</p>
  <pre data-verify="python"><code>from axiom import Matcher

matcher = Matcher(n=8, mode="basic")
updates = [
    ("insert", 0, 1),
    ("insert", 1, 2),
    ("insert", 4, 5),
    ("delete", 0, 1),
]

for operation, u, v in updates:
    getattr(matcher, operation)(u, v)
    assert matcher.maximal()

print(matcher.matching())
print(matcher.partners())
print(matcher.stats())</code></pre>

  <h2>Replay a deterministic trace</h2>
  <p>Generate a valid insert/delete sequence from a seeded random generator, save it with your experiment, and replay it against another matcher.</p>
  <pre data-verify="python"><code>import random
from axiom import Matcher
from axiom.simulation import random_updates, replay

rng = random.Random(7)
trace = list(random_updates(n=50, steps=100, rng=rng))

matcher = Matcher(50, mode="multilevel")
replay(matcher, trace)
assert matcher.maximal()
print(matcher.size(), matcher.stats()["matching_size"])</code></pre>

  <h2>Compare modes</h2>
  <p><code>compare</code> runs the same seeded workload in independent worker processes. Compare throughput only under the same graph size, update count, and seed.</p>
  <pre><code>from axiom.parallel import compare

if __name__ == "__main__":
    results = compare(n=100, updates=1_000, seed=42)
    for mode, result in results.items():
        print(mode, round(result.updates_per_sec), result.is_maximal)</code></pre>
  <p class="doc-note">Run this from a Python file or module. The main guard is required on platforms that use the <code>spawn</code> multiprocessing start method.</p>

  <h2>Command line smoke test</h2>
  <pre><code>$ axiom --n 20 --mode basic --updates 200 --seed 42
Completed 200 updates in 0.001s
Final edges: 12
Matching size: 8
Maximal: True</code></pre>

  <h2>Integration checklist</h2>
  <ul><li>Validate update ordering at your input boundary.</li><li>Persist the update trace if you need reproducibility.</li><li>Assert maximality in staging and benchmark runs.</li><li>Pin the Axiom version and record the selected mode.</li><li>Do not share one matcher instance across concurrent writers.</li></ul>
</DocLayout>
